# From Local Coin Flips to Global Network Transitions

Every potential edge is one independent coin flip, yet the resulting graph changes character at sharply predictable scales. Begin with the [Erdős–Rényi model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erd%C5%91s%E2%80%93R%C3%A9nyi_model), then use the sampler below to see how the model is generated.

{{algorithm:0}}

## Two transitions, two questions

At $ppprox 1/n$, a component occupying a positive fraction of the graph is born. At $ppprox (\log n)/n$, the final isolated vertices disappear and the graph becomes connected. The first asks whether *many* vertices communicate; the second asks whether *every* vertex communicates.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the branching-process explanation</summary>
Component exploration resembles a [Galton–Watson process](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galton%E2%80%93Watson_process) with mean offspring $np$. Below one it dies quickly; above one it survives with positive probability $ho$, where $ho=1-e^{-npho}$.
</details>

## The sharp connectivity window

Write $p=(\log n+c)/n$. The limiting probability of connectivity is $e^{-e^{-c}}$. Adjust $c$ and watch the complete limiting curve.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the Poisson argument</summary>
The expected number of isolated vertices is $n(1-p)^{n-1}	o e^{-c}$. Its factorial moments converge to those of a Poisson random variable with mean $e^{-c}$. Other disconnected components are negligible, so connectivity has the same limit as having zero isolated vertices: $e^{-e^{-c}}$.
</details>

## Counting local patterns

For a family of edge patterns $\mathcal T$, the expected number present is $\sum_{T\in\mathcal T}p^{|T|}$. For triangles this is $ inom n3p^3$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the first- and second-moment principles</summary>
The [union bound](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boole%27s_inequality) gives $\Pr(X>0)\le\mathbb E[X]$. Conversely, $\Pr(X=0)\le\operatorname{Var}(X)/(\mathbb E[X])^2$. Thus a vanishing expectation proves absence, while a diverging expectation with controlled variance proves presence.
</details>

## Run the complete experiment

The final program compares finite simulations with the connectivity limit, scans the giant-component transition, and checks the exact triangle expectation.

{{demo:2}}

For further background, see the overview of [random graphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Random_graph) and the [second moment method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_moment_method).

